From: John Crispin <john@phrozen.org>
Subject: [PATCH] keep build paths out of the configure string

configure.in compiles the whole configure command line into the module as
DRV_TAPI_CONFIGURE_STR, and drv_tapi_linux.c prints it through a proc
file. The command line holds the absolute paths of the build machine,
among them --enable-kernelbuild, --with-ifxos-incl, CPPFLAGS and LDFLAGS.
The module therefore depends on the directory it was built in, and two
builds in different directories produce different modules.

The published snapshot module of lantiq/xway holds, for example,
--enable-kernelbuild=/builder/shared-workdir/build/build_dir/...

Drop every argument that holds a path. The feature flags, which are the
part a reader of the proc file wants, stay. The loop repeats the idiom
that the file already uses for CONFIGURE_OPTIONS.

Signed-off-by: John Crispin <john@phrozen.org>
---
 configure.in | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/configure.in
+++ b/configure.in
@@ -86,7 +86,20 @@ AH_BOTTOM([
 #endif /* _DRV_CONFIG_H_ */
 ])

-AC_DEFINE_UNQUOTED([DRV_TAPI_CONFIGURE_STR], ["$ac_configure_args"], [configure options string])
+# The string below is printed through a proc file. An absolute path of the
+# build machine has no meaning on the target, and it makes the module depend
+# on the directory it was built in. Keep the arguments that hold no path.
+CONFIGURE_STR=''
+for c_arg in $ac_configure_args; do
+   case $c_arg in
+      *"/"*)
+         ;;
+      *)
+         CONFIGURE_STR="$CONFIGURE_STR $c_arg"
+         ;;
+   esac
+done
+AC_DEFINE_UNQUOTED([DRV_TAPI_CONFIGURE_STR], ["$CONFIGURE_STR"], [configure options string])

 dnl enable linux kernel 2.6.x support (Make sure AC_PROG_CC is defined before to set $CC!)
 AM_CONDITIONAL(KERNEL_2_6, false)
